it's over a hundred years since world  war one but the images of the front  lines haven't changed a bit out here  and like the christmases of the great  war the troops on both sides dug into  the trenches are holding out for the new  year  holding out and hoping they'll make it  these are the ukrainian armies trenches  in the east of the country  they snake along a 250 mile demarcation  line across a narrow no man's land a  russian-backed separatist militia and  they're equally well dug in  the narrow walkways and tunnels  the near silence all add to an eerie  sense of foreboding  and there are constant reminders that  this is a war  just pass the sign saying beware sniper  there's been a ceasefire here since 2015  but it's constantly violated with both  sides accusing the other  but russia's now involved building up  troops even threatening an invasion  the ukrainian soldiers think it's a  bluff  but they say they're ready anyway  [Music]  with heat seeking cameras the soldiers  attempt to monitor the troops and  equipment on the separatist side  their front line is that row of trees  it's barely a hundred meters away  well despite all the diplomatic moves  conversations between the united states  and russia  and the increasing evidence to show that  there are more and more russian troops  at least gathering not far away  it doesn't actually make much difference  to these guys  they're sort of stuck in these  world wall one style trenches  and they've just got to wait and see if  if they're attacked or not  they've literally got no choice  these lines aren't as remote as they  appear however they're actually near  where some people still live  these front-line towns in the industrial  heartlands of eastern ukraine a  tough-looking reminders of the soviet  past  those who couldn't leave are stuck in  the midst of a continuing conflict  the streets are pretty empty a few  cyclists venture out in the mornings  amongst them 13 year old daniel  kacchenko  every day he gets on his bike to go  fishing  he heads to the reservoir that cools a  nearby power station  his uncle taught him to build this net  before he too left because of the war  what daniel catches he sells to local  markets  with the money he feeds the town's  homeless dogs abandoned in the  evacuation  left behind like this little boy and his  mum  the fighting has gone on for half of his  life  it's just part of it now  of course  in truth there are thousands of daniels  all along the demarcation line  caught up in a conflict they can't  escape from  learning to live with a war that hasn't  stopped  for eight christmases in a row  mr ramsey sky news eastern ukraine
 

